[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Architecture and Modular Design of JUnit 5"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"JUnit 5 represents a complete redesign of the industry-standard testing framework for Java. Unlike its monolithic predecessor JUnit 4, JUnit 5 is built upon a modular architecture that separates the foundation for running tests from the mechanisms used to write them."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"The Three-Module Architecture"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The JUnit 5 framework is primarily composed of three distinct sub-projects: JUnit Platform, JUnit Jupiter, and JUnit Vintage."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"JUnit Platform: Serves as the foundation for launching testing frameworks on the JVM. It defines the TestEngine API for developing testing frameworks that run on the platform."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"JUnit Jupiter: Contains the new programming model and extension model for writing tests and extensions in JUnit 5."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"JUnit Vintage: Provides a TestEngine for running legacy JUnit 3 and JUnit 4 based tests on the new platform."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Exploring JUnit Jupiter Components"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"JUnit Jupiter introduces powerful new annotations and features, such as nested tests, parameterized tests, and a robust extension model that replaces older runner mechanisms."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.assertEquals;

public class CalculatorTests {
    @Test
    void addition() {
        assertEquals(2, 1 + 1);
    }
}"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"The Role of TestEngine API"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"The JUnit Platform provides a Service Provider Interface (SPI) known as TestEngine. This allows third-party testing frameworks, like Spock, Cucumber, or TestNG, to plug directly into the JUnit ecosystem and be executed uniformly via IDEs and build tools like Maven and Gradle."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Backward Compatibility with JUnit Vintage"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Migration from JUnit 4 to JUnit 5 does not require an immediate rewrite of the entire codebase. The junit-vintage-engine allows existing JUnit 4 tests to execute alongside modern JUnit Jupiter tests seamlessly."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"// JUnit 4 style test running via JUnit Vintage Engine
import org.junit.Test;
import static org.junit.Assert.assertTrue;

public class LegacyTest {
    @Test
    public void legacyCheck() {
        assertTrue(true);
    }
}"},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"By decoupling the test execution engine from the developer-facing APIs, JUnit 5 achieves high extensibility, modularity, and backward compatibility, ensuring a future-proof testing ecosystem for Java applications."}]
